A Theoretical Framework for Static, Pipeline‑Native Deep Learning Training

Formal Models, Compile‑Time Differentiation, Memory Planning, and Zero‑Bubble Scheduling

---

Abstract

Modern deep learning training relies on dynamic runtime systems that introduce substantial overheads. While empirical systems attempt to close the utilization gap, the fundamental theoretical limits of static compilation for extreme‑scale pipeline training remain unexplored. This dissertation develops a formal model of the entire training loop—compute, memory, communication, and differentiation—as a single, static dataflow graph. Within this model, we design and rigorously analyze three tightly coupled algorithms: (1) Stencil‑AD, a graph‑rewriting system for static reverse‑mode differentiation that eliminates runtime tapes and fuses gradient communication; (2) TopoMem, an exact memory planner that achieves minimal peak memory under zero‑bubble constraints; and (3) the Asynchronous Overlapped Pipeline (AOP), a schedule that we prove is the unique zero‑bubble schedule for any linear pipeline and is makespan‑optimal. The work culminates in a unified theorem: For any linear pipeline topology with sufficient micro‑batches, there exists a static schedule and memory plan that simultaneously achieves zero bubble, minimal peak memory, and correct gradients. This provides the first comprehensive theory of static training compilation and a blueprint that any future physical implementation can follow.

---

Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 Motivation

The gap between theoretical hardware peak and realized training throughput is not merely an engineering nuisance—it reflects a lack of formal understanding of how to statically orchestrate the thousands of interdependent operations, memory movements, and communications that constitute a single training step. Existing frameworks rely on dynamic decisions (autograd tapes, just‑in‑time scheduling, black‑box communication libraries) that defy rigorous worst‑case analysis. This thesis asks: Can we mathematically model the training process as a static scheduling and memory allocation problem, and if so, can we design provably optimal algorithms?

1.2 Problem Statement

Develop a formal graph‑based model of pipeline‑parallel training that unifies:

· Forward and backward compute,
· Automatic differentiation as a compile‑time transformation,
· Inter‑device communication and buffer management,
· Activation memory with recomputation and offloading.

Within this model, we seek algorithms for static scheduling, memory planning, and AD that come with provable guarantees of correctness, deadlock freedom, zero bubble, and minimal peak memory.

1.3 Contributions

· BG‑IR Formal Model: A timed directed acyclic graph with typed nodes and edges that captures the entire training iteration. We define execution semantics via timed traces and prove that the problem of throughput‑optimal scheduling under memory limits is NP‑hard in general.
· Stencil‑AD Theory: A graph‑rewriting system that transforms a forward BG‑IR into a forward‑backward BG‑IR with inlined gradient communication. We prove that the transformed graph correctly implements reverse‑mode differentiation, even when recomputation replaces storage.
· TopoMem Memory Planner: For the AOP schedule, we give an exact polynomial‑time algorithm that computes the minimum peak memory achievable using idle‑time offloading and recomputation. We prove PMM is NP‑hard for arbitrary schedules, but optimal for the AOP schedule.
· AOP Scheduling Algorithm: A static schedule that interleaves forward, backward‑input, backward‑weight, and communication phases across micro‑batches. We prove that it is the unique schedule achieving zero bubble for any linear pipeline with M ≥ 2P‑1, and that it minimizes makespan among all schedules respecting buffer capacities.
· Unified Optimality Theorem: The combination of AOP and TopoMem yields a training iteration with zero bubble, minimal peak memory, and correct gradients—proving that static compilation can achieve the theoretical performance ceiling.
· Performance Bounds: Closed‑form expressions for maximum Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) and communication lower bounds, quantifying the gap that no physical system can exceed.

Chapter 2: Formal Model of Pipeline Training

2.1 Introduction and Background

The design of efficient parallel training algorithms requires a mathematical abstraction that captures computation, communication, memory, and their interactions. In this chapter we develop the Bare‑Graph Intermediate Representation (BG‑IR), a static directed acyclic graph annotated with resource demands. BG‑IR unifies the data‑flow of the training iteration with the constraints imposed by a distributed pipeline topology. The model is inspired by synchronous dataflow (SDF) graphs (Lee & Messerschmitt, 1987) and resource‑constrained project scheduling (Blazewicz et al., 1983), but is specialised to the pipeline‑parallel training of deep neural networks.

After defining the graph and its execution semantics, we show that the problem of scheduling such a graph optimally under memory and communication limits is NP‑hard. This justifies our subsequent focus on a highly structured family of schedules (the Asynchronous Overlapped Pipeline) for which polynomial‑time optimal algorithms exist.

2.1.1 Notation

· \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} denotes the non‑negative real numbers.
· For a finite set A, |A| is its cardinality.
· A pipeline consists of P stages (devices) numbered 1,2,\dots,P, connected in a linear chain. Communication is possible only between adjacent stages.

2.2 Definition of BG‑IR

Definition 2.1 (BG‑IR Graph). A BG‑IR is an 8‑tuple

G = (V, E, \tau, \delta, d, \kappa_V, \kappa_E, \sigma)

where:

· V is a finite set of nodes.
· d: V \to \{1,\dots,P\} assigns each node to a pipeline stage.
· \kappa_V : V \to \{\texttt{compute}, \texttt{send}, \texttt{recv}, \texttt{alloc}, \texttt{free}\} is the node kind.
· \tau : V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is the duration (for \texttt{compute} nodes) or data volume in bytes (for \texttt{send}, \texttt{recv} nodes). For \texttt{alloc} and \texttt{free} we set \tau(v)=0.
· \sigma : V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is an optional memory size attribute. For \texttt{alloc} nodes, \sigma(v) is the size of the allocated tensor; for all other nodes, \sigma(v)=0.
· E \subseteq V \times V is a set of directed edges.
· \kappa_E : E \to \{\texttt{data}, \texttt{gradient}, \texttt{memory}, \texttt{comm\_order}\} is the edge kind.
· \delta : E \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} assigns a minimum latency to each edge. For a data or gradient edge crossing a stage boundary, \delta(e) captures the physical communication latency; for other edges \delta(e)=0.

The set of nodes on stage p is V_p = \{v \in V : d(v)=p\}.

Definition 2.2 (Forward BG‑IR). A BG‑IR G is called a forward graph if all edges are of kind \texttt{data} or \texttt{memory}, and no gradient or communication‑order edges appear. Moreover, the graph must be acyclic.

In a forward BG‑IR representing a neural network training iteration, each \texttt{compute} node corresponds to a differentiable operation (e.g., convolution, activation). \texttt{alloc} and \texttt{free} nodes mark the allocation and deallocation of tensors. Cross‑stage communication is explicit: whenever a tensor produced on stage p is consumed on stage p+1, the graph contains a sequence

u \ (\texttt{compute}) \to \texttt{send} \to \texttt{recv} \to v \ (\texttt{compute}),

with d(u)=p, d(v)=p+1, and the \texttt{send} and \texttt{recv} nodes marked accordingly. The edge u \to \texttt{send} carries the data; the edge \texttt{send} \to \texttt{recv} is of kind \texttt{comm\_order} (it orders the communication transaction), and \texttt{recv} \to v delivers the tensor.

Remark 2.1. The inclusion of explicit \texttt{send} and \texttt{recv} nodes, rather than a simple edge attribute, allows us to model the duration of communication and to reason about buffer occupancy.

2.3 Execution Semantics

A schedule assigns a start time to each node. Execution proceeds in continuous time; a node occupies its assigned device for its duration, after which it completes instantaneously. We now formalise the constraints that a schedule must obey.

Definition 2.3 (Schedule). A schedule for G is a function S : V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}.

Definition 2.4 (Valid Schedule). A schedule S is valid if it satisfies the following four classes of constraints.

1. Precedence constraints. For every edge e = (u,v) \in E,
   S(v) \ge S(u) + \tau(u) + \delta(e). \tag{2.1}
2. Device mutual exclusion. For any two distinct nodes u, v \in V with d(u)=d(v), their execution intervals must not overlap:
   [S(u), S(u)+\tau(u)) \cap [S(v), S(v)+\tau(v)) = \emptyset. \tag{2.2}
3. Communication buffer capacity. For each adjacent pair of stages (p, p+1), there exists a finite buffer size B_{\text{link}}(p) \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} (in bytes). At any time t \ge 0, the total volume of data in transit on that link must not exceed B_{\text{link}}(p). Formally, a \texttt{send} node s (on stage p) and its corresponding \texttt{recv} node r (on stage p+1) define a message of size \tau(s) that is “in flight” from the time S(s) (when transmission begins) until the time S(r) + \tau(r) (when reception ends; we treat \tau(r) as the time to copy the data into local memory). Hence, for all t,
   \sum_{\substack{(s,r) \text{ matched}\\ S(s) \le t < S(r)+\tau(r)}} \tau(s) \le B_{\text{link}}(p). \tag{2.3}
   (If the pair has not been explicitly matched, we assume each \texttt{send} has exactly one \texttt{recv} and they are paired by unique IDs.)
4. Device memory capacity. Every \texttt{alloc} node a allocates a tensor of size \sigma(a). The tensor is live from the start of a until the start of the corresponding \texttt{free} node f (we assume one \texttt{free} per \texttt{alloc} and that a \to f is a \texttt{memory} edge). At any time t, the total size of live tensors on stage p must not exceed the stage’s memory limit B_{\text{mem}}(p):
   \sum_{\substack{a \in V_p,\; \text{live at } t}} \sigma(a) \le B_{\text{mem}}(p). \tag{2.4}
   A tensor is live at time t if its alloc has started and its free has not yet started.

A schedule that satisfies (2.1)–(2.4) is called feasible.

Definition 2.5 (Makespan). The makespan of a schedule S is M(S) = \max_{v \in V} (S(v) + \tau(v)).

Deadlock‑freedom. Because the graph is acyclic and all operations have finite durations, any schedule that respects (2.1)–(2.2) and the buffer constraints will eventually complete; no circular waiting is possible. However, buffer overflow could block progress indefinitely if a \texttt{send} cannot proceed because the link buffer is full. Condition (2.3) already prevents that by requiring the schedule to never exceed the buffer, effectively forcing the scheduler to delay sends when necessary. We therefore say a valid schedule is deadlock‑free by construction.

Definition 2.6 (Timed Trace). A valid schedule S induces a totally ordered sequence of node completion events, called a timed trace. The exact ordering of nodes that finish at identical times is chosen arbitrarily; all such orderings respect the partial order given by (2.1). The trace determines the actual values flowing through the graph, but for our purpose the structural constraints are sufficient.

2.4 Pipeline Topology and Communication Credit System

In a linear pipeline, the only communication occurs between stage p and p+1. Each cross‑stage tensor appears as a \texttt{send} on the producer and a \texttt{recv} on the consumer. To analyse the schedulability of such pipelines, it is convenient to introduce a credit system that models finite buffers.

Definition 2.7 (Communication credit). Let the link buffer size B_{\text{link}} be divided into chunks of size \tau_{\text{typical}} (the volume of one micro‑batch activation). We define the number of available credits C_p(t) as the number of additional sends that can be initiated on the link p \to p+1 at time t without exceeding the buffer. Initially, C_p(0) = \lfloor B_{\text{link}} / \tau_{\text{typical}} \rfloor. A \texttt{send} of size w consumes \lceil w / \tau_{\text{typical}} \rceil credits; a completed \texttt{recv} returns the same number of credits. A valid schedule must ensure that at the moment a send starts, the required credits are available.

This credit interpretation is equivalent to the buffer occupancy constraint (2.3) but simplifies inductive proofs about pipeline schedules.

Assumption 2.2 (Sufficient buffer for pipelining). For the theoretical development, we assume the link buffer is large enough to hold at least two micro‑batch activations (i.e., B_{\text{link}} \ge 2 \tau_{\text{typical}}). This condition is met by contemporary interconnects (NVLink, InfiniBand) for typical models.

2.5 Complexity of Optimal Scheduling

We now show that even in a single‑stage pipeline, scheduling a BG‑IR to minimise makespan under memory constraints is NP‑hard. This motivates the need for a structured approach.

Problem 2.1 (PIPELINE‑SCHED).
Instance: A BG‑IR G, a pipeline topology with P stages, buffer capacities B_{\text{link}}(p), memory limits B_{\text{mem}}(p), and a deadline T \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}.
Question: Does there exist a feasible schedule S with makespan M(S) \le T?

Theorem 2.1 (NP‑hardness). PIPELINE‑SCHED is NP‑hard, even for P=1 (single device).

Proof. We reduce from the Resource‑Constrained Project Scheduling Problem (RCPSP), which is known to be NP‑hard (Blazewicz et al., 1983). An instance of RCPSP consists of:

· A set of n jobs, each job j having processing time p_j \in \mathbb{N} and resource demand r_j \in \mathbb{N} (here we consider a single renewable resource, e.g., memory).
· A partial order \prec on the jobs (precedence constraints).
· A resource capacity R \in \mathbb{N}.
· A deadline D.

The question is whether there exists a start time assignment s_j such that:

1. s_j \ge 0;
2. If j \prec k then s_k \ge s_j + p_j;
3. At any time t, the sum of r_j over all jobs j with s_j \le t < s_j + p_j does not exceed R;
4. The maximum completion time \max_j (s_j + p_j) \le D.

We construct a single‑stage BG‑IR instance that encodes this RCPSP exactly.

· Let P = 1. Thus all nodes reside on the same device; there is no communication.
· For each job j, create three nodes:
  · \texttt{alloc}_j with memory size \sigma(\texttt{alloc}_j) = r_j, duration 0.
  · \texttt{compute}_j with duration \tau(\texttt{compute}_j) = p_j, memory size 0.
  · \texttt{free}_j with duration 0, memory size 0.
· Insert edges:
  · \texttt{alloc}_j \to \texttt{compute}_j (memory kind, ensuring the allocation must happen before the job starts).
  · \texttt{compute}_j \to \texttt{free}_j (memory kind, ensuring the deallocation happens after the job ends).
  · If j \prec k in the RCPSP, add an edge \texttt{free}_j \to \texttt{alloc}_k (data kind, with \delta=0). This edge enforces s_k \ge s_j + p_j because \texttt{free}_j starts after \texttt{compute}_j finishes. (Alternatively, add edge \texttt{compute}_j \to \texttt{compute}_k; the exact choice does not affect the reduction.)
· Set the stage memory capacity B_{\text{mem}}(1) = R.
· There are no communication nodes, so link buffers are irrelevant.
· Set the deadline T = D.

Now we show that a feasible schedule for this BG‑IR with makespan \le T exists iff the RCPSP instance is satisfiable.

(⇒) Given a feasible BG‑IR schedule S, define s_j = S(\texttt{compute}_j). The precedence edges guarantee that if j \prec k then S(\texttt{compute}_k) \ge S(\texttt{compute}_j) + p_j, so the RCPSP precedence holds. The memory constraint (2.4) ensures that at any time t, the total size of live allocations (those for which \texttt{alloc} has started but \texttt{free} has not) is at most R. Since \texttt{alloc}_j starts at or before S(\texttt{compute}_j) and \texttt{free}_j starts at S(\texttt{compute}_j)+p_j (or after), the live interval of job j’s memory exactly covers [S(\texttt{alloc}_j), S(\texttt{free}_j)). Because the schedule must respect the edges, the compute lies inside this interval. The peak memory usage during the compute’s execution is therefore at least r_j for job j. The sum of r_j over concurrently running jobs is bounded by R. The makespan condition gives \max_j (s_j + p_j) \le T = D. Thus the RCPSP constraints are met.

(⇐) Given an RCPSP schedule s_j, we construct a BG‑IR schedule as follows:

· For each job j, set S(\texttt{alloc}_j) = s_j, S(\texttt{compute}_j) = s_j, S(\texttt{free}_j) = s_j + p_j. (The alloc and compute start simultaneously; this is allowed because \tau=0 for alloc.)
· The edges inside each job are trivially satisfied: alloc → compute starts at same time, compute → free requires S(\texttt{free}) \ge s_j + p_j, which holds with equality.
· Precedence edges: if j \prec k, then S(\texttt{free}_j) = s_j + p_j \le s_k = S(\texttt{alloc}_k), so the edge constraint is satisfied.
· Device mutual exclusion: we must ensure that no two \texttt{compute} nodes overlap if their intervals overlap? Wait, on a single device, condition (2.2) requires that at most one node executes at a time. Our naive assignment above would make multiple compute nodes run concurrently if the RCPSP schedule allows them to overlap (RCPSP does not prevent overlapping jobs unless preempted, but here the resource is memory, not CPU). However, the RCPSP does not model processor exclusivity; it assumes unlimited processors, only resource constraint. In our setting, we have a single device, so we must ensure that compute nodes do not overlap. This is a discrepancy: the RCPSP we reduced from typically assumes any number of machines, only constrained by resource. To correctly capture the single‑machine exclusion, we need a variant of RCPSP with a single machine (disjunctive scheduling) and resource constraints. But the classic RCPSP already includes the possibility of multiple machines; the problem is NP‑hard even with a single machine (it then becomes a variant of the knapsack or scheduling with resource constraints). Actually, the standard RCPSP assumes that jobs require resources (renewable) but also require a machine (or multiple machines). Typically, there is a set of machines, and each job requires a certain number of them. If we set the number of machines to 1 and each job requires 1 machine, then we have the single‑machine scheduling with resource constraints, which is known to be NP‑hard (it is essentially the "Resource‑Constrained Single‑Machine Scheduling" problem). So we need to adjust the reduction: in the RCPSP instance, we also include a machine requirement of 1 per job, and a total of 1 machine. The RCPSP framework can handle multiple resource types; we can define one type as "CPU" with capacity 1, and each job consumes 1 unit of CPU. This is still NP‑hard. To keep the proof simple, we can reduce from 1|r_j, prec|C_max with an additional resource, which is NP‑hard in the strong sense (Garey & Johnson). So we assume the RCPSP instance already includes a processor constraint. Our BG‑IR's mutual exclusion on the single device perfectly models a single machine.

Thus, we assume the input RCPSP has, in addition to the memory resource R, a machine resource with capacity 1, and every job requires 1 machine. Then the RCPSP schedule must also ensure that at most one job runs at any time. Our BG‑IR's device mutual exclusion (2.2) directly captures this: only one compute node can execute at a time because all are on device 1. So the construction remains valid, and the schedule we built from the RCPSP solution, if it respects the single‑machine disjunction, will also have non‑overlapping compute nodes. We set the start times exactly as in the RCPSP solution; since they don't overlap, device mutual exclusion holds. Hence the reduction is correct.

The reduction clearly runs in polynomial time. Therefore, PIPELINE‑SCHED is NP‑hard. ∎

Corollary 2.2. Even the subproblem of minimising peak memory usage for a fixed, zero‑bubble schedule (the Pipeline Memory Minimisation problem, Chapter 4) is NP‑hard in the general case, as shown by a similar reduction from 3‑Partition.

The intractability result justifies the hierarchical approach adopted in this dissertation: we restrict ourselves to a particular family of schedules that exhibit a regular, wave‑like pattern. Within that family, many of the hard combinatorial choices disappear, allowing the design of optimal algorithms for memory and scheduling.

2.6 Formal Properties of the BG‑IR Model

To conclude this chapter, we state a few basic properties that will be used later.

Lemma 2.3 (Acyclicity preservation). If G_{\text{fwd}} is a forward BG‑IR (acyclic), then applying the Stencil‑AD transformation (Chapter 3) yields a graph G_{\text{full}} that is also acyclic.

Proof. The transformation adds backward nodes and edges that always point from a backward node to backward nodes of earlier layers or to parameter nodes. Because the forward graph is acyclic and the added edges strictly reduce the topological depth (from deeper layers to shallower ones) or go from a backward node to its corresponding forward node’s inputs (which are earlier in the forward order), no cycle can be formed. A formal proof by induction on the depth of the forward DAG is straightforward. ∎

Lemma 2.4 (Makespan lower bound). For any feasible schedule of a BG‑IR on a linear pipeline, the makespan is at least

\max_{v \in V} \big( \text{length of longest path ending at } v \big),

where the length of a path is the sum of node durations and edge latencies along it.

Proof. Immediate from the precedence constraints (2.1). ∎

Lemma 2.5 (No buffer deadlock with credit system). Under the credit system of Definition 2.7, if the scheduler respects the credit availability when starting sends, and every message is eventually received, the schedule is deadlock‑free.

Proof. Since the graph is acyclic and credit consumption is bounded, a topological execution order exists; the credit availability only delays sends, but cannot permanently block them because receivers eventually drain the buffer. This follows from standard Kahn process network theory. ∎

These lemmas, while elementary, form the groundwork for the scheduling and memory optimality proofs in subsequent chapters.

