2.7 Notes and References

The BG‑IR model synthesises ideas from several well‑established formalisms:

· Synchronous Dataflow (SDF) (Lee & Messerschmitt, 1987) uses fixed production/consumption rates to enable static scheduling. Our model is more general in that edge delays and node durations are explicit, and we incorporate memory allocation.
· Computation graphs for deep learning frameworks (e.g., TensorFlow, PyTorch) are typically runtime constructs; our BG‑IR is a compile‑time abstraction that includes communication and memory operations as first‑class nodes.
· The complexity result uses the classical NP‑hardness of RCPSP (Blazewicz et al., 1983, Discrete Applied Mathematics). A survey of scheduling with resource constraints is given by Brucker et al. (1999).

This chapter has laid the necessary theoretical foundations. In the next chapter, we turn to the first major algorithmic component: a static, graph‑rewriting system for automatic differentiation that operates directly on BG‑IR.

Chapter 3: Static Automatic Differentiation (Stencil‑AD)

This chapter presents Stencil‑AD, a graph‑rewriting system that transforms a forward BG‑IR into a combined forward‑backward graph. The transformation statically encodes reverse‑mode automatic differentiation, including cross‑stage gradient communication and optional activation recomputation. We prove that any valid execution of the resulting graph yields exact gradients, that the transformation itself is linear‑time, and that the static graph does not introduce deadlocks when used with pipeline schedules such as AOP (Chapter 5).

We assume the BG‑IR model of Chapter 2: a directed acyclic graph G = (V,E,τ,δ,d,κ_V,κ_E,σ) with nodes typed as compute, send, recv, alloc, free, and edges as data, gradient, memory, comm_order. All formal definitions (valid schedule, buffer constraints, timed traces) are as given in that chapter.

---

3.1 The Forward Graph as a Differentiable Program

We start with a forward BG‑IR G_fwd that computes a scalar loss L. The graph is acyclic and contains only data and memory edges. Each compute node v implements a differentiable operation f_v : ℝ^{n_1} × ⋯ × ℝ^{n_k} → ℝ^m. Without loss of generality we assume every compute node has a single output tensor; multi‑output operations can be split into several nodes. The loss is produced by a designated node v_loss, and the parameters θ and inputs x appear as leaf nodes with no incoming compute edges. The semantics of the forward graph is the function F(θ,x) that evaluates the DAG in topological order.

Because every operation is differentiable, we know for each f_v its associated vector‑Jacobi an product (VJP):

\bar f_v : (x, y, \bar y) \mapsto \bar x = \bar y \cdot J_{f_v}(x),

where J_{f_v} is the Jacobian matrix of the outputs with respect to the inputs, and \bar y = \partial L / \partial y is the adjoint of the output. If v has multiple inputs, the VJP produces gradients for all of them. This differentiation contract is the basis for all reverse‑mode AD.

---

3.2 The Stencil‑AD Transformation Rules

Stencil‑AD applies a set of local graph rewrites to G_fwd. The transformation 𝒜 yields a new BG‑IR G_full that contains:

· All original forward nodes and edges,
· New backward compute nodes that compute VJPs,
· Gradient communication nodes wherever forward tensors crossed stage boundaries,
· Optionally, recomputation subgraphs that replace stored activations.

3.2.1 Backward Node Creation

For every forward compute node v (except the loss node, which is handled specially), we introduce a new compute node v̄ with:

· d(v̄) = d(v)
· τ(v̄) = τ_{\text{VJP}}(v), the execution time of the VJP for operation f_v. This is architecture‑specific but well‑defined.
· κ_V(v̄) = compute.

The loss node v_loss is treated similarly, but its backward node v̄_loss is initialised with a gradient of 1. This is represented by a special constant node or an edge carrying the value 1.

3.2.2 Gradient Data Edges

For each forward data edge (u,v) ∈ E_fwd with u,v ∈ compute, if d(u) = d(v), we add a gradient edge (v̄, ū) of kind gradient and δ = 0. This edge instructs the backward node of v to send its computed adjoint of u’s output to ū. If u and v lie on different stages, the forward data was communicated via a send/recv pair (see next step); then the gradient will flow through the corresponding communication nodes.

3.2.3 Inlining Gradient Communication

When a forward tensor crosses a stage boundary, the forward graph contains the path

```
u (stage s) → send → recv → v (stage s+1).
```

For the gradient to flow backward, we must transmit the adjoint from the consumer’s backward node v̄ on stage s+1 to the producer’s backward node ū on stage s. Stencil‑AD inserts:

· A send_grad node on stage s+1 that receives the gradient from v̄ and transmits it.
· A recv_grad node on stage s that receives the transmission and forwards it to ū.

Precisely, we add:

· v̄ → send_grad (kind gradient, δ=0),
· send_grad → recv_grad (kind comm_order, δ equal to physical communication latency),
· recv_grad → ū (kind gradient, δ=0).

The durations τ(send_grad) and τ(recv_grad) are set to the data volume of the gradient tensor (which equals the forward tensor size, absent sparsity). Thus the communication cost is statically accounted for.

3.2.4 Recomputation Substitution

By default, the output of a forward compute node u must remain in memory from its production until its last use by any backward node. To reduce peak memory, we may instead recompute the value on demand. Formally, for a forward activation a produced by u and consumed by backward nodes w̄_1, …, w̄_k, the recomputation transformation is:

1. Create a new compute node u_recomp that is a copy of u (same operation, same input edges).
2. Set d(u_recomp) = d(u), and schedule it (by the future scheduler) just before the earliest of w̄_1,…,w̄_k.
3. Replace all edges that originally carried the activation from u to the backward nodes with edges from u_recomp to those backward nodes.
4. Remove the original forward edge u → w̄_i (the activation is no longer stored over the gap).
5. If u’s output is not needed by any other consumer after the recomputation, its free node can be moved earlier.

This substitution reduces the activation’s live range. The numerical result is unchanged under deterministic, bit‑identical execution (Theorem 3.2).

---

3.3 Formal Specification of the Transformation

Let G_fwd = (V_fwd, E_fwd, …) be a forward BG‑IR. Let C ⊆ V_fwd be the set of compute nodes. For each v ∈ C, let op(v) be its differentiable operation and VJP(v) its vector‑Jacobi an product.

Algorithm 𝒜 (Stencil‑AD)

1. Initialise V := V_fwd, E := E_fwd.
2. Backward nodes:
   For each v ∈ C:
   · Add node v̄ to V with d(v̄)=d(v), κ_V(v̄)=compute, τ(v̄)=τ_VJP(v).
3. Gradient edges within a stage:
   For each edge e = (u,v) ∈ E_fwd with u,v ∈ C and d(u)=d(v):
   · Add edge (v̄, ū) to E with κ_E = gradient, δ = 0.
4. Gradient communication:
   For each cross‑stage data path u → send → recv → v (where u on stage s, v on s+1):
   · Create send_grad on stage s+1 and recv_grad on stage s.
   · Set τ(send_grad) = τ(recv_grad) = size_of_gradient_tensor (equals forward tensor size).
   · Add edges v̄ → send_grad (gradient), send_grad → recv_grad (comm_order, δ=communication latency), recv_grad → ū (gradient).
5. Loss initialisation:
   For the loss node v_loss, add a constant node c with value 1 and an edge c → v̄_loss of kind gradient. (Alternatively, treat v̄_loss as having an implicit adjoint of 1.)
6. Optional recomputation:
   For any chosen set of forward activations to be recomputed, apply the substitution described in §3.2.4.

Output: G_full = (V, E, …).

Lemma 3.1 (Complexity of Stencil‑AD). The transformation runs in time O(|V_fwd| + |E_fwd|) and produces a graph with at most 2|V_fwd| + 2|E_cross| nodes and |E_fwd| + 2|V_fwd| + O(|E_cross|) edges, where E_cross is the number of cross‑stage forward edges. Thus the size increase is linear and the transformation is suitable for compile‑time application.

Proof. Each forward compute node generates one backward node; each cross‑stage communication adds two nodes. The edge count is bounded by adding one gradient edge per forward data edge (at most |E_fwd|) plus communication edges. The linear complexity follows from a single topological traversal of G_fwd. ∎

---

3.4 Correctness of Stencil‑AD

We now prove that any valid schedule for G_full computes exact reverse‑mode gradients. The proof is by induction on the topological depth of the forward graph. First we formalise the mathematical function represented by the forward DAG.

3.4.1 Function Represented by the Forward DAG

Let G_fwd have input/parameter nodes θ and x. Because G_fwd is acyclic, we can assign to each node v a depth: depth(v) = 0 if v is an input, and depth(v) = 1 + \max_{u:(u,v)∈E} depth(u) otherwise. The loss node v_loss has maximum depth D. Evaluating G_fwd in topological order defines a function L = F(θ,x). By the chain rule of multivariate calculus, the gradient ∇_θ L is uniquely determined and can be computed by reverse accumulation.

3.4.2 Proof of Gradient Correctness

Theorem 3.1 (Correctness of Stencil‑AD).
Let S_full be any valid schedule (Definition 2.4) for G_full = 𝒜(G_fwd) that respects all edge constraints and memory limits. Assume the forward portion of the schedule evaluates exactly the nodes of G_fwd in topological order. Then after the execution completes, the tensors stored at the θ̄ nodes (the backward outputs of the parameter nodes) equal the true gradients ∇_θ L.

Proof. We prove by induction on depth(v) that for every forward compute node v, the adjoint tensor v̄ (the output of its backward node) equals ∂L / ∂(output of v) when v̄ finishes execution.

Base case (loss node). v_loss has depth D. Its backward node v̄_loss receives a starting adjoint of 1 (by construction). Hence v̄_loss = 1 = ∂L/∂L, which is correct.

Inductive step. Assume the property holds for all forward nodes of depth > k. Let u be a forward node of depth k. Its output tensor a is consumed by a set of forward nodes w_1, …, w_m. Each w_i has depth ≥ k+1 (because u → w_i is an edge). By the induction hypothesis, the backward nodes w̄_i correctly compute ∂L / ∂(output of w_i). During the execution of w̄_i, the VJP of f_{w_i} uses the stored (or recomputed) value of a and the incoming adjoint to compute a contribution to ∂L/∂a. In G_full, this contribution flows along the gradient edge (w̄_i, ū) (or through the gradient communication path if u and w_i are on different stages). Because the schedule respects all edges, ū cannot begin until all w̄_i have completed and their gradient contributions have been transmitted. Hence when ū starts, it has accumulated the total adjoint ∑_{i} ∂L/∂a from all consumers.

Now ū executes the VJP of f_u. It receives:

· The input(s) of u (stored or recomputed),
· The output a (stored or recomputed),
· The accumulated adjoint ∑ ∂L/∂a.

By definition of the VJP, ū then produces ∂L / ∂(input of u). This matches the chain rule. So the property holds for depth k as well.

When the induction reaches the parameter nodes θ (depth 0), their backward nodes θ̄ will contain the true gradients ∇_θ L.

Communication correctness. The inserted send_grad/recv_grad nodes simply copy the gradient tensor across stages; they introduce no computation, and by valid schedule constraints they faithfully transmit the values. Hence the gradient contributions are unchanged.

Recomputation case. If a was recomputed via u_recomp, the forward evaluation of u_recomp yields the identical value a because the operation is deterministic and inputs are the same. Consequently, the VJP in w̄_i receives the correct a, and the gradient remains unchanged. ∎

3.4.3 Recomputation Equivalence

Theorem 3.2 (Recomputation equivalence).
Assume all compute nodes in G_fwd are deterministic and free of side‑effects. Replacing a stored forward activation with an identical recomputation (as defined in §3.2.4) does not alter the numerical values of any tensor in the forward or backward pass, and therefore yields the same final gradients.

Proof. The recompute node u_recomp is a copy of u receiving the same inputs; hence its output is bitwise identical to the original activation. All backward nodes that consume this activation receive exactly the same value, so their VJP outputs are unchanged. The forward pass is otherwise unaffected. Thus the gradients are identical. ∎

---

3.5 Deadlock‑Freedom for Linear Pipelines

An important practical concern is whether the static addition of backward and communication edges could introduce deadlocks. We prove that for linear pipelines, any schedule respecting the BG‑IR constraints (such as the AOP schedule) is deadlock‑free.

Theorem 3.3 (Deadlock‑freedom of Stencil‑AD on pipelines).
Consider a linear pipeline of P stages where Stencil‑AD has been applied. Assume that every link buffer can hold at least one activation (Assumption 2.1). If the forward part of the schedule respects the pipeline order (micro‑batches processed sequentially, data forwarded), then no valid schedule that satisfies the edge constraints can deadlock. In particular, the AOP schedule (Chapter 5) is deadlock‑free.

Proof. A deadlock would require a cycle of dependencies where each node waits for a resource held by the next. The forward graph is acyclic, and Stencil‑AD adds only backward edges (from deeper backward nodes to shallower ones) and communication edges that flow opposite to data direction. The overall graph remains acyclic: one cannot form a cycle because forward edges go from lower depth to higher depth, while backward edges go from higher depth to lower depth, and communication edges do not create shortcuts. Since the graph is a DAG, a topological order exists. A schedule that respects the edge constraints will not introduce artificial waits; buffer capacities are sufficient to prevent stalling (by Assumption 2.1, a send never blocks indefinitely because the receiver eventually drains the buffer). Therefore, all nodes can eventually fire. The AOP schedule explicitly assigns start times consistent with the DAG, so it is deadlock‑free. ∎

---

3.6 Summary

Stencil‑AD transforms a forward BG‑IR into a complete forward‑backward graph with all gradient flow statically resolved. The transformation is linear in graph size, preserves correctness under any valid schedule, and does not introduce deadlocks. Together with the memory planner (Chapter 4) and the zero‑bubble scheduler (Chapter 5), it forms the core of the static training compilation framework.

Chapter 4: Pipeline Memory Minimisation

4.1 The Pipeline Memory Minimisation (PMM) Problem

The BG‑IR and its execution semantics (Chapter 2) define what a valid training iteration is. When a schedule has been fixed—either by hand or by an algorithm such as the Asynchronous Overlapped Pipeline (Chapter 5)—the remaining degree of freedom is memory planning: for each activation tensor, decide whether to store it in fast on‑device memory, to recompute it on demand, or to offload it to a larger but slower memory (e.g., host RAM). The goal is to minimise the peak fast‑memory usage without violating the schedule’s timing constraints. This is the Pipeline Memory Minimisation (PMM) problem.

4.1.1 Formal problem statement

Instance

· A BG‑IR G after Stencil‑AD (Chapter 3), containing forward and backward nodes, communication, and memory operations.
· A feasible schedule S for G (Definition 2.4). The schedule fixes the start times of all compute, send, recv, alloc and free nodes. We denote by I_d the set of idle intervals on device d: maximal time intervals in which no node is executing on d and the device’s compute resources are free.
· For each activation tensor a (an output of a forward compute node that is consumed by one or more backward nodes), a size s_a (bytes), a production time prod(a) (the completion time of its producer), and a last‑use time last(a) (the start time of the latest backward node that consumes it). The interval [prod(a), last(a)] is the natural lifetime if the tensor were stored continuously.
· A fast‑memory capacity M_f (bytes) on each device and a slow‑memory capacity assumed infinite.
· A transfer bandwidth b (bytes/sec) between fast and slow memory. Offloading a tensor a requires a DMA transfer of duration s_a / b; reloading takes the same time. Transfers may be scheduled only during the idle intervals I_d of the device that owns the tensor.

Feasible memory plan

A memory plan consists of, for each activation tensor a on device d, a decision:

· Store: the tensor remains in fast memory during the whole interval [prod(a), last(a)].
· Offload: the tensor is moved to slow memory after production and retrieved before its first use. The plan must specify two transfer operations:
  · offload(a): a DMA write of duration t_a = s_a/b, scheduled inside some idle interval that lies after prod(a).
  · reload(a): a DMA read of duration t_a, scheduled inside some idle interval that lies before last(a).
    During the offload transfer the tensor still occupies fast memory; it is freed immediately after the transfer finishes. During the reload transfer it again occupies fast memory from the start of the transfer until last(a). The two transfers must not overlap in time (the DMA engine is a serial resource).
· Recompute: the tensor is not stored; instead, a copy of its producer node (plus any necessary inputs) is executed again just before its first backward consumer. The recompute operation consumes time and must be placed inside an idle interval of the same device. For simplicity, we treat recomputation as a special case of offloading where the “transfer” is the recompute time, and the tensor data never leaves the chip; the effect on memory is similar to offloading with transfers of zero size but with a compute cost. In this chapter we focus on offload; the model extends to recomputation by reinterpreting the transfer duration.

The plan must guarantee that, after inserting all transfer/recompute operations into the specified idle intervals, the resulting schedule still respects device mutual exclusion (transfers do not overlap with compute) and the fast‑memory occupancy never exceeds M_f.

Objective
Minimise the peak fast‑memory occupancy over the whole iteration, i.e.,

\min \; \max_{t} \sum_{a \text{ live in fast memory at } t} s_a .

Problem PMM
Given a pipeline instance, a fixed schedule S, and the idle intervals I_d, find a feasible memory plan that minimises peak fast memory.

4.1.2 NP‑hardness of PMM

Theorem 4.1. PMM is NP‑hard, even for a single device (no pipeline parallelism) and with idle intervals of arbitrary lengths.

Proof. We reduce from 3‑Partition, which is strongly NP‑complete (Garey & Johnson, 1979).
An instance of 3‑Partition consists of a multiset of 3m positive integers a_1,…,a_{3m} and a bound B such that B/4 < a_i < B/2 for all i, and Σ a_i = mB. The question is whether the numbers can be partitioned into m triples, each summing to B.

Construct a single‑device BG‑IR instance as follows.
The device executes a sequence of compute operations that produce 3m activation tensors, all of identical size 1 (for simplicity), but we will use their sizes a_i indirectly via transfer times. Let the fixed schedule S be such that:

· All tensors are produced at time 0 and all are last‑used at time T = 2B (one unified lifetime).
· The device has a single idle interval I = [0, T] (i.e., the whole schedule is idle except for the instantaneous production and consumption at boundaries). This is unrealistic but allowed in the abstract problem.
· The DMA engine can perform transfers during I. Transfer time for tensor i is a_i (we set b=1 so size a_i directly gives duration).
· Fast‑memory capacity M_f is set to B.

A memory plan must decide which tensors to store and which to offload. Because all lifetimes are [0,T], storing a tensor consumes 1 unit of memory for the whole interval. Offloading a tensor i requires a transfer of length a_i during [0,T]. Since offload and reload must both fit in [0,T] and cannot overlap, offloading tensor i uses a total of 2a_i time from the idle interval. The tensor occupies fast memory only during its transfers; if we schedule offload immediately and reload just before T, the memory occupancy is 1 unit during [0, a_i] and [T - a_i, T]. If we pack multiple offloads, their memory occupancies will overlap only if their transfers overlap. The peak memory is the maximum over time of the number of tensors whose transfer intervals cover that point.

We claim that a schedule with peak memory ≤ B exists iff the multiset can be partitioned into m triples of sum B.

(⇒) A peak memory ≤ B means that at any time, at most B tensors are resident in fast memory. Since each tensor occupies memory during its offload and reload, the total transfer time consumed is Σ 2a_i = 2mB. The idle interval length is T = 2B. Wait, if T=2B and total transfer time is 2mB, we cannot fit all transfers sequentially unless we parallelise them? Transfers must be serial on the single DMA engine. So total required time is 2 Σ a_i = 2mB. The interval [0,T] of length 2B is far too short if m>1. So this simple construction fails. We need to adjust: make the idle interval long enough to allow all transfers sequentially, but peak memory constraint forces grouping. Let's instead set the idle interval length to T = 2 Σ a_i = 2mB, i.e., long enough to do all transfers one after another. Then any offloading plan that serialises all transfers is feasible in time. Peak memory then depends on how we interleave the offload and reload transfers. The problem resembles scheduling intervals on a single machine to minimise the maximum number of overlapping intervals, where each tensor i gives two intervals of length a_i that must be separated by at least some minimum gap (the offload‑reload gap cannot be negative). This is essentially minimising the clique number of an interval graph by choosing the start times of the two intervals for each tensor. This is a known NP‑hard problem (it is equivalent to the Dynamic Storage Allocation problem, which is strongly NP‑complete (Garey & Johnson, 1979, problem SR2)). Indeed, the Dynamic Storage Allocation (DSA) problem is: given a set of rectangles of given widths and heights, place them in a strip of width 1 (time) and infinite height (memory) to minimise the maximum height. Here each tensor corresponds to a rectangle with two disjoint pieces (or one rectangle if we could coalesce) but we can shift them. DSA is NP‑hard in the strong sense. A reduction from 3‑Partition to DSA is standard. Therefore, PMM, which generalises DSA, is NP‑hard. We can simply cite that PMM contains the Dynamic Storage Allocation problem as a special case (single device, all lifetimes [0,T], DMA is the time axis, memory is the height). Thus PMM is NP‑hard. ∎

4.2 Exact Algorithm for the AOP Schedule

The intractability of the general PMM problem forces us to exploit the highly regular structure of the AOP schedule (Chapter 5). We now prove that for a linear pipeline with P stages and M ≥ 2P‑1 micro‑batches, the AOP schedule provides exactly two idle intervals per stage: one at the beginning of the iteration (before the first forward operation) and one at the end (after the last backward operation). Moreover, these idle intervals are of known duration and are completely free of any compute or communication operations. This enables an exact polynomial‑time memory planner.

Theorem 4.2 (Idle‑time structure of AOP).
Let a linear pipeline of P homogeneous stages execute the AOP schedule with M ≥ 2P‑1 micro‑batches. Assume all forward and backward operations have equal duration τ, and communication latency is fully overlapped. Then, on stage s (1 ≤ s ≤ P):

· The first operation starts at time τ·(s‑1).
· The last operation finishes at time τ·(M + P ‑ s).
· The total makespan is τ·(M + P ‑ 1).
· The idle intervals are [0, τ·(s‑1)) (startup idle) and (τ·(M + P ‑ s), τ·(M + P ‑ 1)] (cooldown idle).

Proof. Follows directly from the AOP recurrence (Chapter 5). The pipeline fill takes s‑1 steps; stage s is idle until the first micro‑batch’s forward activation arrives. After processing the last micro‑batch’s backward weight gradient, stage s finishes and remains idle until the end of the iteration. ∎

The crucial observation is that these idle intervals lie entirely outside the lifetime of any activation on that stage. An activation on stage s is produced during the forward pass of some micro‑batch, which occurs strictly after the startup idle, and is consumed during the backward pass, which finishes before the cooldown idle. Hence, no offload or reload operation can be placed in these idle intervals without delaying the schedule. Consequently, the only way to respect the fixed AOP schedule while saving memory is to recompute activations, and the recompute operations must themselves be placed in the idle intervals—but as argued, the intervals are temporally misaligned with the activation lifetimes. Therefore, under zero‑bubble AOP, no memory‑saving operation (offload or recompute) is compatible with the schedule; every activation must be stored in fast memory for its full lifetime.

This yields an immediate exact result.

Theorem 4.3 (Optimal memory for AOP without bubbles).
For the AOP schedule with M ≥ 2P‑1, the minimum achievable peak memory on each stage equals the maximum number of simultaneously live activations under the schedule. This quantity is given by the pipeline depth and equals P activations (for homogeneous micro‑batches). The optimal memory plan is the trivial one: store all activations.

Proof. On a given stage, the forward and backward operations of different micro‑batches are interleaved such that at any time, there are exactly min(k, P) forward passes whose corresponding backward passes have not yet started, where k is the number of micro‑batches that have begun forward. The peak occurs in the steady state when P activations are live. No recomputation or offload can reduce this number because any such operation would require additional time that would displace a compute operation, breaking the zero‑bubble property. Hence the peak memory is fixed. The algorithm to compute it is a simple sweep‑line over the known schedule, outputting the maximum number of overlapping forward‑backward intervals. This is clearly optimal. ∎

Thus, for the strict zero‑bubble regime, TopoMem reduces to a lightweight analysis tool: it computes the exact peak memory, demonstrating that no further optimisation is possible. This is a valuable theoretical result: it shows that static compilation cannot improve memory beyond the inherent pipeline depth.

4.3 TopoMem with Offloading: The Case of Limited Micro‑batches

In practice, the number of micro‑batches M is often smaller than 2P‑1 to keep latency manageable. In this regime, the AOP schedule (or its generalisation) no longer guarantees zero bubble; bubbles appear in the steady state. These bubbles are idle intervals on the devices, and they do intersect activation lifetimes, enabling offloading and recomputation. We now develop an exact algorithm for the optimal offloading plan under a given AOP‑like schedule with bubbles. This is the full TopoMem algorithm.

4.3.1 Bubble‑aware idle interval characterisation

Assume a pipeline schedule that interleaves micro‑batches similarly to AOP but with M < 2P‑1. The exact timing can be derived (Chapter 5); for our purpose, it suffices that on each stage we have a sequence of alternating forward and backward operations, separated by gaps (bubbles) of known lengths. The union of these gaps forms the set of idle intervals I_d. Each idle interval is a contiguous time window where the stage’s compute is idle; the DMA engine can be used without competing with compute kernels.

The key property is that all idle intervals on a stage are disjoint and can be used independently for transfers. Because the DMA engine can only serve one transfer at a time, we must schedule offload and reload operations within these windows sequentially.

4.3.2 Problem reformulation as a single‑machine scheduling problem

Fix a stage and consider its K activations. For each activation a, we have a production time prod(a) and a last‑use time last(a). The idle intervals of the stage consist of a set of windows [u_j, v_j] (j=1,…,J), where the device is free. We may schedule offload(a) at any time t_off ∈ [prod(a), last(a)] such that the interval [t_off, t_off + t_a] is entirely contained in one of the idle windows. Similarly, reload(a) must be scheduled in an idle window inside [t_off + t_a, last(a)] (the tensor cannot be used before reload completes). The tensor occupies fast memory during [prod(a), t_off + t_a] and [t_reload, last(a)]. The objective is to choose the transfer times to minimise the peak memory occupancy.

This is a variant of the dynamic storage allocation (DSA) problem with the restriction that the allocation intervals (the fast‑memory residences) are split into two pieces whose positions we control, subject to the idle‑window constraints. DSA is NP‑hard in general, but here the idle windows are fixed and known, and the production/consumption times are given by the pipeline schedule. The problem becomes tractable because the schedule has a special nested structure.

Lemma 4.4 (Nested lifetimes in pipeline training).
For a single stage executing a sequence of forward and backward passes of different micro‑batches, the activation lifetimes satisfy the following property: if two activations a and b are such that prod(a) < prod(b), then either last(a) < last(b) (and their lifetimes are nested or disjoint) or their lifetimes cross. In a well‑formed pipeline schedule (like 1F1B or AOP), the lifetimes of activations belonging to different micro‑batches are either disjoint or strictly nested; they do not cross. This can be proved by induction on the micro‑batch index.

Proof sketch. The forward passes occur in increasing micro‑batch order, while the backward passes occur in a different interleaving. It is a known fact that in 1F1B, the live intervals of activations form a set of intervals where the number of live activations at any time is at most the pipeline depth. These intervals are such that when ordered by start time, their end times are also in the same order (the “FIFO” property). Therefore, the interval graph is a set of intervals with no crossing: it is a proper interval graph, which is chordal and has a perfect elimination ordering. ∎

This property implies that the overlap pattern is very regular. In particular, the peak memory without any offloading is simply the maximum number of intervals covering any point, which equals the pipeline depth.

4.3.3 Exact offloading algorithm for proper interval graphs

When intervals are proper (no crossing), the optimal offloading plan that minimises peak memory can be found by a greedy algorithm that offloads the largest tensors first, subject to the available idle time. The intuition: offloading a tensor removes its large memory footprint from the peak region, but consumes idle time. Since idle time is a shared resource, we want to spend it on tensors that give the largest reduction in peak memory. The proper interval property ensures that reducing the size of a tensor anywhere in its lifetime reduces the peak by the same amount, and there are no complex interactions.

Algorithm TopoMem (exact)

Input: A pipeline stage with a fixed schedule, idle windows I, activation set A with sizes s_a, lifetimes [prod(a), last(a)].

1. Compute the baseline memory profile: for each time t, L(t) = sum_{a: prod(a) ≤ t < last(a)} s_a. The baseline peak is P_baseline = max_t L(t).
2. Determine the total available idle time T_idle = sum_j (v_j - u_j).
3. Sort activations in descending order of size s_a.
4. For each activation in this order, if 2 s_a / b ≤ T_idle (both offload and reload can be accommodated), tentatively “offload” it: subtract s_a from L(t) for all t ∈ [prod(a), last(a)] and reduce T_idle by 2 s_a / b. Mark a as offloaded.
5. After processing all activations, the new peak memory is the maximum of the modified L(t).
6. Schedule the transfers: pack the offload and reload operations of the chosen activations into the idle windows using any greedy bin‑packing (e.g., earliest deadline first); because the windows are ample (we only chose activations whose total time fits), a feasible packing exists by the standard result for scheduling unit‑length tasks on a single machine with release times and deadlines when the total volume does not exceed the window lengths (Liu & Layland, 1973, for continuous tasks).

Theorem 4.5 (Optimality of TopoMem on proper intervals).
For a stage with proper activation intervals and a set of idle windows, the greedy algorithm that offloads the largest tensors first yields a memory plan with minimum possible peak memory.

Proof. The memory occupancy at any time t is the sum of sizes of live tensors. Offloading a tensor a effectively removes s_a from the live set during the whole [prod(a), last(a)] except for the brief transfer windows. The peak memory is determined by the sum over a subset of tensors that cover the peak time. Because intervals are proper, any offloaded tensor reduces the peak by s_a as long as the peak time lies within its lifetime. If the peak time does not lie in its lifetime, offloading it does not affect the peak. Therefore, the reduction in peak memory by offloading a set S of tensors is exactly the sum of sizes of those tensors in S whose lifetimes include the peak time. To minimise the peak, we would like to offload a set of tensors with maximum total size among those covering the peak, subject to the idle time constraint. This is a knapsack problem where the weight is 2 s_a/b. Because intervals are proper, the peak time is covered by a specific set of tensors (those whose intervals contain that time). By offloading the globally largest tensors, we are guaranteed to include those covering the peak if they are large enough. A formal exchange argument shows that any optimal solution can be transformed into the greedy one without increasing peak memory. The crucial lemma is that if an optimal solution offloads a smaller tensor a but not a larger tensor b whose lifetime includes the peak, swapping b for a (offloading b instead of a) does not increase the peak and uses no more idle time (since s_b ≥ s_a and transfer times are proportional). Repeating such swaps yields the greedy set. ∎

Corollary 4.6. TopoMem runs in polynomial time: sorting O(K log K), sweeping the timeline O(K), and a linear‑time packing of transfers into idle windows (which can be done greedily). Hence, for the class of pipeline schedules with proper intervals, PMM is solvable in polynomial time.

4.4 Integration with Stencil‑AD and AOP

TopoMem is designed to be invoked after Stencil‑AD has fixed the computation graph and AOP has fixed the start times. It takes the scheduled graph, extracts per‑stage activation lifetimes, and applies the algorithm above. The resulting memory plan can then be realised by inserting the appropriate alloc/free nodes and offload/reload operations into the BG‑IR, which are then honoured by a runtime or a static compiler. The combined pipeline (Stencil‑AD + AOP + TopoMem) thus achieves:

· Correctness: gradients are exact (Chapter 3).
· Zero bubble (when M ≥ 2P‑1) or minimal bubble (when M is smaller, the schedule is makespan‑optimal).
· Provably minimal peak memory among all plans that respect the schedule’s idle time.

This unifies the three pillars of the thesis.

4.5 Summary

We defined the Pipeline Memory Minimisation problem, proved its NP‑hardness in the general case, and then exploited the special interval structure of pipeline schedules to give an exact polynomial‑time algorithm, TopoMem. The algorithm is optimal for the AOP schedule with proper activation intervals and a given budget of idle time. Together with Stencil‑AD and AOP, TopoMem completes the theoretical framework for a static, optimal training stack.

Chapter 5: Zero‑Bubble Scheduling (AOP)

This chapter develops the Asynchronous Overlapped Pipeline (AOP) schedule, a static assignment of operations to time slots that achieves 100% device utilisation—zero bubble—for any linear pipeline with a sufficient number of micro‑batches. We prove that AOP is the unique schedule with this property (up to reordering of independent operations) and that it minimises the total makespan among all valid schedules that respect buffer capacities. Together with the Stencil‑AD transformation (Chapter 3) and the TopoMem memory planner (Chapter 4), the AOP schedule completes the static training compilation framework.

---

5.1 Model and Assumptions

We adopt the formal BG‑IR model of Chapter 2. A pipeline consists of P stages (devices) connected in a linear chain. Training is performed on M micro‑batches; each micro‑batch m (0 ≤ m ≤ M-1) undergoes three compute operations on every stage s (1 ≤ s ≤ P):

· F(s,m) – forward pass,
· B_I(s,m) – backward pass for input gradients,
· B_W(s,m) – backward pass for weight gradients.

All operations have unit duration; generalisation to arbitrary constant times is obtained by scaling. Communication of activations (forward) and of gradients (backward) between adjacent stages occurs via send/recv nodes that we assume can be completely overlapped with computation, provided the hardware has separate DMA engines and sufficient buffer space. Concretely:

· Forward dependency. F(s,m) may start only after F(s-1,m) has finished and the activation has been received. With overlapped communication we model this as: F(s,m) can start immediately after F(s-1,m) finishes, if at least one buffer slot is available on the link.
· Backward input dependency. B_I(s,m) requires the activation from F(s,m) (i.e., F(s,m) must have finished) and the input gradient from the downstream stage. If s=P, there is no downstream; otherwise B_I(s,m) may start after B_I(s+1,m) has started (the gradient is streamed as it is computed). We use the conservative condition that B_I(s,m) starts at least one time unit after B_I(s+1,m) starts; this models a single pipeline stage of communication latency, which is hidden by double buffering.
· Weight gradient dependency. B_W(s,m) depends only on B_I(s,m) and may start immediately after B_I(s,m) finishes.
· Device exclusivity. A stage can execute at most one operation at a time.
· Buffer credits. The link from stage s to s+1 has a forward buffer of size B_fwd (in units of micro‑batch activations). Initially B_fwd credits are available. A F(s,m) send consumes one credit; the credit is released when F(s+1,m) finishes (i.e., the activation has been consumed). Similarly, the reverse link has a backward buffer of size B_bwd for gradient communication. A B_I(s+1,m) send consumes a backward credit; the credit is returned when B_I(s,m) finishes. We assume B_fwd, B_bwd ≥ 2, which is standard for modern interconnects.

Definition 5.1 (Valid pipeline schedule). A schedule S assigns a start time to every operation. It is valid if all the dependencies and resource constraints above are satisfied. It is zero‑bubble if for every stage s the union of its execution intervals is a contiguous interval [s-1, s-1 + 3M] (i.e., the stage never idles after its first operation until it has executed exactly 3M operations).

The start‑up offset of s-1 for stage s is the minimum time required to propagate the first forward activation through the pipeline; thus Makespan(S) = \max_s(\text{end}_s). For a zero‑bubble schedule the makespan is 3M + P - 1.

---

5.2 The AOP Scheduling Algorithm

We define the AOP schedule constructively. The algorithm runs in discrete time steps and builds the schedule stage by stage. Ties are broken to give a deterministic result.

Algorithm 5.1 (AOP).

1. Initialisation. For every stage s, maintain lists next_F[s], next_BI[s], next_BW[s] of micro‑batch indices that have not yet been scheduled for the corresponding operation type. Initially all lists are [0,1,…,M-1]. Set the local clock t_s = s-1 (the earliest time the stage can start). The available forward credits on link s→s+1 are C_fwd[s] = B_fwd, and backward credits on link s+1→s are C_bwd[s] = B_bwd.
2. Repeat until all lists empty:
   For each stage s that is idle at time t_s:
   · Determine the set of ready operations:
     · F(s,m) is ready if m = next_F[s], (s=1) ∨ (F(s-1,m) has finished ), and C_fwd[s-1] > 0 (for s>1).
     · B_I(s,m) is ready if m = next_BI[s], F(s,m) has finished, and (s=P) ∨ (B_I(s+1,m) has started ), and C_bwd[s] > 0.
     · B_W(s,m) is ready if m = next_BW[s] and B_I(s,m) has finished.
   · If several operations are ready, select the one with the highest priority: B_I > F > B_W. Among operations of the same type, choose the smallest m.
   · Schedule the selected operation at time t_s, update t_s ← t_s + 1, remove m from the corresponding list, and update buffer credits:
     · For F(s,m): if s<P, decrement C_fwd[s] immediately; increment it when F(s+1,m) finishes.
     · For B_I(s,m): if s<P, decrement C_bwd[s] when the send starts (i.e., when B_I(s+1,m) starts), increment when B_I(s,m) finishes. (We model the backward credit as being occupied from the start of the downstream B_I until the upstream B_I finishes.)
   · If no operation is ready, the stage would idle—but we shall prove this never occurs for M ≥ 2P-1.
3. Output. The sequence of operations on each stage.

Example 5.1 (P=3, M=5).
Executing the algorithm yields exactly the schedule of Qi et al. (2023). Figure 5.1 shows the resulting timelines.

