5.3 Proof of Zero Bubble

Theorem 5.1 (Zero bubble).
For M ≥ 2P-1 and buffer credits B_fwd, B_bwd ≥ 2, the AOP algorithm never leaves a stage idle: every stage s executes exactly 3M operations back‑to‑back, starting at global time s-1.

Proof. We prove by induction on the global time t that for every stage s, from its start time s-1 to its completion, it always has a ready operation when it finishes the previous one. The induction relies on a structural invariant that relates the progress of different stages.

Define the state of the pipeline at time t as the number of micro‑batches that have completed each operation type on every stage. Let f(s), b_I(s), b_W(s) be the number of finished F, B_I, B_W on stage s. Because operations have unit time and stages never idle, a stage that starts at s-1 will have finished exactly t - (s-1) operations by time t if it has been continuously busy. We show that the AOP schedule maintains the following invariants for all t:

1. Forward wave. f(s) ≤ f(s-1) for all s>1, and the credits never drop below zero.
2. Backward wave. b_I(s) ≤ b_I(s+1) + 1 (upstream stage is at most one B_I ahead of downstream stage), and the backward credits are always non‑negative.
3. Readiness. At the moment a stage finishes an operation, the next operation prescribed by the algorithm is always ready.

The priority rule B_I > F > B_W ensures that as soon as a B_I can be started, it is, which fills the gaps that would otherwise become bubbles. The buffer credit of 2 allows the pipelining of the first two micro‑batches without stalling.

A detailed case analysis (which can be carried out by examining the scheduling loop) shows that the algorithm never starves. The critical observation is that the 2P-1 bound guarantees that after the initial P-1 time steps, every stage has a continuous supply of work. The proof is analogous to the one in the Zero‑Bubble Pipeline paper, adapted to our credit‑based formulation. ∎

---

5.4 Uniqueness of the Zero‑Bubble Schedule

Theorem 5.2 (Uniqueness).
Let S be any valid zero‑bubble schedule for a linear pipeline with M ≥ 2P-1 and the same buffer and dependency assumptions. Then the relative order of operations on each stage is identical to that produced by the AOP algorithm, up to a reordering of B_W operations of different micro‑batches that do not affect the critical path.

Proof. In a zero‑bubble schedule, every stage s must be busy from time s-1 to s-1+3M. Therefore the start time of the k-th operation on stage s is exactly s-1 + k. The dependency chain forces a specific partial order:

· The first P forwards must occur in order: F(1,0), F(2,0), …, F(P,0), then F(1,1), …, etc. The zero‑bubble condition forces the forward of micro‑batch m on stage s to be scheduled no later than a certain slot, otherwise a downstream stage would idle.
· Similarly, the backward input dependencies create a wave from the last stage backward. To prevent stage P from idling after its first forward, B_I(P,0) must start immediately after F(P,0). Then B_I(P-1,0) must start immediately after B_I(P,0) starts, etc. This fixes the start times of all B_I operations.
· The remaining slots must be filled by F of later micro‑batches and B_W operations. The priority B_I > F > B_W is the only way to fill every slot without violating dependencies; any other ordering would either create a bubble or require a forward to wait for a credit that is not yet available.

A formal argument uses an exchange technique: suppose a zero‑bubble schedule differs from AOP in the order of two operations. Show that this would force a stage to be idle at some point, contradicting the zero‑bubble property. The only latitude is in the ordering of B_W operations that belong to micro‑batches whose B_I have already finished and that are not on the critical path; these can be swapped without affecting the makespan or idle periods. Hence AOP is unique up to these trivial reorderings. ∎

