5.6 Discussion

The AOP schedule, combined with the credit system, realises the ideal of static pipeline compilation. The conditions M ≥ 2P-1 and B_fwd, B_bwd ≥ 2 are mild and met in large‑scale training. The uniqueness result shows that the schedule is essentially forced, giving implementors a clear target. The makespan optimality closes the gap between theory and practice: no dynamic runtime can beat the performance of this static schedule.

Together with Stencil‑AD (Chapter 3) and TopoMem (Chapter 4), the AOP schedule forms the third pillar of the dissertation. The next chapter combines them into a unified optimality theorem and derives closed‑form performance bounds.


Chapter 6: Theoretical Performance Analysis

---

6.1 Introduction

The previous chapters established the correctness (Stencil‑AD), memory optimality (TopoMem), and scheduling optimality (AOP) of a fully static training stack. In this chapter we turn to the quantitative performance limits: what is the maximum achievable throughput, expressed as Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU), of any pipeline‑parallel training iteration? We derive closed‑form expressions for the MFU of the AOP schedule and of conventional schedules (1F1B, GPipe), and we compare them analytically. The analysis yields an absolute upper bound on the MFU of any static schedule, set by the unavoidable communication time along the pipeline’s critical path. The results quantify the benefit of zero‑bubble scheduling and the memory‑optimised micro‑batch sizing enabled by TopoMem.

---

6.2 Model of Execution Time

We adopt the BG‑IR model from Chapter 2 and the operation decomposition of Chapter 5. Training proceeds over M micro‑batches on a linear pipeline of P identical stages. Each stage possesses a peak computational throughput C (FLOPs/s) and is equipped with a DMA engine that can communicate independently of computation. The operations of one micro‑batch have the following durations:

· Forward pass: T_F (seconds)
· Backward pass for input gradients: T_{BI}
· Backward pass for weight gradients: T_{BW}

The total compute time for one micro‑batch on one stage is

T_{\text{comp}} = T_F + T_{BI} + T_{BW}.

The total number of floating‑point operations in one micro‑batch (forward and backward combined) is F_{\text{micro}}, so that T_{\text{comp}} = F_{\text{micro}} / C when the stage is fully utilised.

Communication between adjacent stages involves sending activations (forward) and gradients (backward). Let the size of an activation tensor be S_{\text{act}} bytes, and the size of the corresponding gradient tensor be the same. The link from stage s to s+1 has bandwidth BW (bytes/s) and a per‑message latency L. The time to transmit one tensor is L + S/BW. With fully overlapped communication (separate DMA, double buffering), the effective communication time that falls on the critical path can be reduced. We denote by T_{\text{comm\_unavoid}} the minimum total communication time that must appear in the makespan under any schedule; this is a lower bound that depends on the aggregate data volume and the pipeline topology.

---

6.3 Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU)

Definition 6.1 (MFU). For a schedule with makespan T_{\text{total}} (the time to complete one training iteration of M micro‑batches), the average Model FLOPs Utilization is

\mathrm{MFU} = \frac{\text{Total FLOPs}}{\text{Total available FLOPs}} = \frac{P \cdot M \cdot F_{\text{micro}}}{P \cdot C \cdot T_{\text{total}}}.

Substituting F_{\text{micro}} = C \cdot T_{\text{comp}}, we obtain the compact form

\boxed{\mathrm{MFU} = \frac{M \cdot T_{\text{comp}}}{T_{\text{total}}}} \tag{6.1}

A schedule’s makespan can be decomposed as

T_{\text{total}} = M \cdot T_{\text{comp}} + T_{\text{bubble}} + T_{\text{comm\_total}},

where T_{\text{bubble}} is the cumulative idle time of all stages not attributed to communication, and T_{\text{comm\_total}} is the total time spent on communication that is not overlapped with computation. Defining the average bubble per micro‑batch \bar{T}_{\text{bubble}} = T_{\text{bubble}} / M and the average exposed communication \bar{T}_{\text{comm}} = T_{\text{comm\_total}} / M, we may rewrite

\mathrm{MFU} = \frac{T_{\text{comp}}}{T_{\text{comp}} + \bar{T}_{\text{bubble}} + \bar{T}_{\text{comm}}}. \tag{6.2}

The quantity \bar{T}_{\text{bubble}} reflects the scheduling inefficiency; \bar{T}_{\text{comm}} captures the unavoidable communication cost that cannot be hidden. A zero‑bubble schedule has \bar{T}_{\text{bubble}} = 0 in the limit M \to \infty, or exactly zero idle time after pipeline start‑up for M \ge 2P-1 (cf. Chapter 5). An ideal communication‑hidden schedule has \bar{T}_{\text{comm}} = 0. Achieving both yields the absolute upper bound \mathrm{MFU} = 1.

---

6.4 Bubble Analysis of Pipeline Schedules

We compare three canonical schedules under the simplifying assumption that all compute operations have equal duration \tau. Thus T_F = T_{BI} = T_{BW} = \tau, and T_{\text{comp}} = 3\tau. Communication is assumed perfectly overlapped (\bar{T}_{\text{comm}}=0), so differences arise solely from scheduling bubbles.

6.4.1 GPipe and 1F1B

GPipe (synchronous, all‑forward then all‑backward):
The forward wave of M micro‑batches takes (M + P - 1)\tau; the backward wave takes (M + P - 1) \cdot 2\tau because the backward pass is unsplit and twice as long. Hence

T_{\text{total}}^{\text{GPipe}} = (M+P-1) \cdot 3\tau = M T_{\text{comp}} + (P-1) T_{\text{comp}}.

Thus T_{\text{bubble}} = (P-1) T_{\text{comp}}, and \bar{T}_{\text{bubble}} = \frac{P-1}{M} T_{\text{comp}}. Plugging into (6.2),

\mathrm{MFU}_{\text{GPipe}} = \frac{T_{\text{comp}}}{T_{\text{comp}} + \frac{P-1}{M} T_{\text{comp}}} = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{P-1}{M}}. \tag{6.3}

1F1B (one‑forward‑one‑backward interleaving, unsplit backward):
This schedule reduces memory but exhibits the same bubble volume as GPipe when T_F = T_B; its makespan is also

T_{\text{total}}^{\text{1F1B}} = M T_{\text{comp}} + (P-1) T_{\text{comp}},

so \mathrm{MFU}_{\text{1F1B}} = \mathrm{MFU}_{\text{GPipe}}.

6.4.2 AOP Schedule

The AOP schedule (Chapter 5) splits the backward pass and achieves zero bubble for M \ge 2P-1. In the unit‑time model, each stage executes 3M operations; the first operation on stage s starts at time s-1, and the stage never idles afterwards. The makespan is therefore

T_{\text{total}}^{\text{AOP}} = 3M + (P-1) = M T_{\text{comp}} + (P-1).

Consequently,

\bar{T}_{\text{bubble}} = \frac{P-1}{M}, \qquad
\mathrm{MFU}_{\text{AOP}} = \frac{T_{\text{comp}}}{T_{\text{comp}} + \frac{P-1}{M}} = \frac{3}{3 + \frac{P-1}{M}}. \tag{6.4}

Comparison. The improvement factor of AOP over 1F1B is

\frac{\mathrm{MFU}_{\text{AOP}}}{\mathrm{MFU}_{\text{1F1B}}} = \frac{1 + \frac{P-1}{M}}{1 + \frac{P-1}{3M}}.

For a deep pipeline (P \gg 1) and a just‑sufficient number of micro‑batches (M = 2P-1), this ratio approaches \frac{1 + 1/2}{1 + 1/6} = \frac{1.5}{1.166...} \approx 1.285. If the baseline 1F1B is additionally constrained by memory, it may be forced to use an even smaller M, widening the gap. For instance, if memory limitations restrict 1F1B to M = P micro‑batches, then \bar{T}_{\text{bubble}} \approx T_{\text{comp}}, giving \mathrm{MFU}_{\text{1F1B}} \approx 0.5, whereas AOP+TopoMem can employ M = 2P-1 with \mathrm{MFU}_{\text{AOP}} \approx 0.86, yielding a ratio of 1.72. Under more extreme memory pressure (e.g., activations too large for M > 4), the improvement can exceed 2.4\times, matching the figures quoted in recent zero‑bubble literature (Qi et al., 2023).

---

6.5 Impact of Communication Bandwidth

So far we assumed perfect overlap (\bar{T}_{\text{comm}} = 0). We now derive a fundamental lower bound on \bar{T}_{\text{comm}} that no physical system can surpass.

Consider the pipeline’s bottleneck link. In a linear pipeline, the link between stage \lceil P/2 \rceil and \lceil P/2 \rceil + 1 carries all activations flowing forward and all gradients flowing backward. The total data that must traverse this link in one iteration is

D_{\text{total}} = M \cdot (S_{\text{act}} + S_{\text{grad}}) = 2M S_{\text{act}}.

Even with perfect overlap, the DMA engine on each stage can only process one transfer at a time. The time required to move this data serially is T_{\text{comm}}^{\text{serial}} = 2M S_{\text{act}} / BW + \text{latency terms}. Some of this communication may be hidden during compute; the maximum hidable time is bounded by the total compute time on the two adjacent stages. The exposed communication \bar{T}_{\text{comm}} must therefore satisfy

\bar{T}_{\text{comm}} \ge \frac{1}{M} \max\!\Big(0,\; T_{\text{comm}}^{\text{serial}} - \text{hidable portion}\Big).

In the worst case (bandwidth‑limited regime), no communication can be hidden, giving

\bar{T}_{\text{comm}} \ge \frac{2 S_{\text{act}}}{BW}.

Inserting this into (6.2) yields an absolute MFU ceiling:

\mathrm{MFU}_{\max} \le \frac{T_{\text{comp}}}{T_{\text{comp}} + \frac{2 S_{\text{act}}}{BW}}. \tag{6.5}

For a concrete example, consider a transformer layer with T_{\text{comp}} = 10 ms, activation size S_{\text{act}} = 2 MB, and an interconnect bandwidth BW = 50 GB/s. Then 2 S_{\text{act}} / BW = 80 ns, which is negligible. However, for larger models or slower interconnects (e.g., S_{\text{act}} = 100 MB, BW = 12.5 GB/s), 2 S_{\text{act}} / BW = 16 ms, and the MFU ceiling drops to 10 / 26 ≈ 0.38. This bound cannot be circumvented by any schedule—it is a fundamental consequence of the data movement required.

---

6.6 Combined Effect of Scheduling and Memory

TopoMem (Chapter 4) minimises peak activation memory, allowing the system to use a larger M than would otherwise be possible. The interplay between M, memory, and MFU is illustrated by the following theorem.

Theorem 6.1 (MFU under memory‑optimal AOP).
Let M_{\text{max}} be the maximum number of micro‑batches that fit in fast memory under the 1F1B schedule, and let M_{\text{AOP}} be the maximum number of micro‑batches that fit under AOP+TopoMem. Under the same memory limit, TopoMem guarantees M_{\text{AOP}} \ge M_{\text{max}}, and in typical settings M_{\text{AOP}} \gg M_{\text{max}}. Consequently, the achievable MFU with AOP+TopoMem can far exceed that of 1F1B, even before accounting for bubble elimination.

Proof sketch. TopoMem exactly minimises peak memory for the AOP schedule, often reducing the per‑micro‑batch memory footprint by offloading or recomputing activations during idle intervals. Because AOP provides ample idle intervals (start‑up and cooldown) that do not exist in 1F1B, memory can be used more efficiently, enabling a larger M within the same hardware budget. The larger M further reduces \bar{T}_{\text{bubble}}, amplifying the MFU advantage. ∎

---

6.7 Numerical Example

Consider a pipeline with P = 48 stages, T_{\text{comp}} = 30 ms, BW = 400 GB/s (NVLink), S_{\text{act}} = 10 MB. Communication overhead is tiny, so we focus on the bubble. Without memory constraints, 1F1B with M = 96 yields \mathrm{MFU} = 1 / (1 + 47/96) ≈ 0.671. AOP with the same M (but M \ge 2P-1 requires M \ge 95, satisfied) gives \mathrm{MFU} = 3 / (3 + 47/96) ≈ 0.859. The relative improvement is 1.28×.

If memory limits force 1F1B to use at most M = 24 micro‑batches, its MFU drops to 1 / (1 + 47/24) ≈ 0.338. AOP+TopoMem may still fit M = 96 micro‑batches, achieving \mathrm{MFU} = 0.859, a 2.54× speedup. Thus the true power of static compilation lies in the joint optimisation of schedule and memory—the central thesis of this dissertation.

---

6.8 Summary

We derived closed‑form MFU expressions for the AOP schedule and for conventional 1F1B/GPipe schedules. The analysis confirms that (i) AOP always outperforms 1F1B when M \ge 2P-1, with an asymptotic improvement factor of up to 3; (ii) communication bandwidth imposes a hard MFU ceiling that no static schedule can exceed; and (iii) the memory savings of TopoMem amplify the throughput advantage by enabling larger M. These bounds provide the theoretical performance ceiling that any future physical implementation of a static training compiler must approach.


Chapter 7: Unified Optimality and the Path to a Static Compiler in C/C++

---

7.1 Synthesis of the Theoretical Framework

The preceding chapters have developed three independent but deeply interdependent results:

1. Stencil‑AD (Chapter 3) provides a static, graph‑rewriting transformation that converts a forward BG‑IR into a combined forward‑backward graph with inlined gradient communication and optional recomputation. The transformation is correct, linear‑time, and deadlock‑free.
2. TopoMem (Chapter 4) solves the Pipeline Memory Minimisation problem exactly for schedules with proper activation intervals (such as those produced by AOP). It computes the minimal peak memory achievable through offloading and recomputation within a given idle‑time budget.
3. AOP (Chapter 5) is a static pipeline schedule that achieves zero bubble and makespan optimality when the number of micro‑batches is sufficiently large. The schedule is essentially unique among zero‑bubble schedules.

This chapter presents the Unified Optimality Theorem, which asserts that when these three algorithms are combined, the resulting static training iteration simultaneously achieves correct gradients, minimal peak memory, and zero‑bubble optimal throughput. We then discuss the practical implications of these results and outline how they lay the foundation for a real static compiler—one that can be implemented in a low‑level, performance‑oriented language such as C or C++. The chapter closes with a survey of extensions and future work.

---

7.2 Unified Optimality Theorem

Theorem 7.1 (Unified Optimality of Static Compilation).
Let a linear pipeline of P homogeneous stages be given, together with a differentiable forward BG‑IR G_fwd (Chapter 2). Apply Stencil‑AD to obtain G_full. Assume the number of micro‑batches satisfies M ≥ 2P-1, the communication buffer capacity is at least 2 (in units of micro‑batch activations), and the per‑stage memory limit B_mem is at least the minimal value returned by TopoMem for the AOP schedule. Then:

1. Feasibility: The AOP schedule S_AOP (Algorithm 5.1) is a valid schedule for G_full.
2. Correctness: When executed according to S_AOP, the gradients computed at the parameters equal the true reverse‑mode gradients ∇_θ L.
3. Zero bubble: Under S_AOP, every stage is continuously busy from its first operation to its last; the total makespan is T_total = 3M + P - 1 (in units of τ).
4. Memory optimality: The peak fast‑memory usage under S_AOP and the memory plan produced by TopoMem is the smallest possible among all valid schedules that achieve zero bubble (and among all schedules that respect the same idle‑time structure).
5. Throughput optimality: No valid schedule for G_full can have a makespan strictly smaller than T_total; hence S_AOP is makespan‑optimal.

Proof. The proof is a direct consequence of the theorems established in earlier chapters.

· Feasibility and zero bubble: Theorem 5.1 guarantees that AOP respects all dependencies and buffer constraints, and achieves zero bubble when M ≥ 2P‑1 and the link buffers have capacity at least 2. Stencil‑AD produces a DAG; AOP is a topological order of that DAG, hence valid.
· Correctness: Theorem 3.1 states that any valid schedule for G_full computes exact gradients. AOP is valid, therefore the gradients are correct.
· Memory optimality: TopoMem (Theorem 4.5) computes the minimal peak memory achievable using offloading within the idle intervals of the AOP schedule. Lemma 4.4 confirms that AOP yields proper activation intervals, and Theorem 4.2 characterises the idle intervals available. Since no zero‑bubble schedule can have more idle time or a different peak (the peak is determined by the pipeline depth), TopoMem’s plan is globally optimal among zero‑bubble schedules.
· Makespan optimality: The lower bound T_total ≥ 3M + P - 1 holds for any valid schedule because stage P cannot start before P‑1 and must execute 3M operations (Theorem 5.3). AOP attains this bound.

Thus the static compilation approach (Stencil‑AD + AOP + TopoMem) simultaneously optimises all three axes. ∎

Corollary 7.2 (Static compilation envelope). The combination Stencil‑AD + TopoMem + AOP defines a Pareto‑optimal point in the space of throughput, memory, and correctness: it is impossible to improve any of these metrics without sacrificing another, within the model’s assumptions.

---

7.3 From Theory to a Static Compiler in C/C++

The theoretical framework described in this dissertation is not only mathematically rigorous; it is also intended for direct translation into a practical compiler. The natural target for such a compiler is a low‑level, systems programming language that offers precise control over memory layout, execution ordering, and hardware resources. C and C++ meet these requirements perfectly.

7.3.1 Why C/C++?

· No Runtime Overhead: C and C++ have no built‑in garbage collection, no virtual machine, and no dynamic typing. This aligns with the static philosophy of the BG‑IR: every operation, memory allocation, and communication is known at compile‑time and can be emitted as plain, statically scheduled C code (or, for device kernels, as CUDA/HIP C++ extensions).
· Deterministic Memory Management: The memory plan produced by TopoMem specifies exactly when each tensor is allocated, offloaded, and freed. This can be materialised as stack‑like allocation in a pre‑allocated arena, using simple pointer arithmetic—no malloc/free at runtime, no fragmentation.
· Compile‑Time Scheduling: The AOP schedule can be unrolled into a fixed sequence of function calls or kernel launches. In C++, this can be expressed as a compile‑time generated table of function pointers, or even as a constexpr array of operations that a lightweight runtime iterates through. The zero‑bubble property guarantees that the sequence is dense; the compiler can further optimise the loop structure.
· Integration with Hardware: C and C++ are the lingua franca of embedded and accelerator programming. The generated code can be linked with vendor‑optimised libraries (cuBLAS, oneDNN) and compiled with aggressive loop optimisations, achieving near‑peak hardware utilisation.

7.3.2 Architecture of a Static Training Compiler

The hypothetical system BareMetalTrain (the physical realisation of the theoretical framework) would be structured as follows:

```
[Model description] 
    → Stencil‑AD (C++ graph‑rewriting library)
        → BG‑IR (static, annotated graph)
    → AOP scheduler (C++ algorithm)
        → schedule (table of operations with timestamps)
    → TopoMem (C++ memory planner)
        → memory map (activation lifetimes and offload commands)
    → Code generator (C++ backend)
        → monolithic C/C++ source file
        → compiled with g++/nvcc → static binary
```

· Graph Representation: The BG‑IR is implemented as a custom graph data structure in C++ with nodes and edges, using templates for different node types. The Stencil‑AD transformation is a series of graph rewrites that run entirely at application startup (or even at model‑design time).
· Scheduling and Memory Planning: AOP and TopoMem are implemented as efficient C++ algorithms (polynomial time) that process the BG‑IR and produce the final schedule and memory plan. These can be invoked offline, before training begins.
· Code Generation: The output is a single C function per device, containing the entire training loop unrolled over micro‑batches. All tensor addresses are statically assigned offsets in a pre‑allocated buffer. Communication is handled via explicit DMA calls (e.g., cudaMemcpyAsync) interleaved exactly as prescribed by the schedule.

The resulting binary is self‑contained, requires no Python interpreter or autograd runtime, and can be deployed on bare‑metal environments—from large clusters to edge devices.

7.3.3 Advantages of a Static Binary

· Predictable Performance: Every operation is scheduled at a known time; there are no queueing delays, no JIT compilations, and no garbage collection pauses. The execution time of the training step is deterministic (assuming no hardware faults).
· Minimal Latency: For real‑time or safety‑critical applications, the static nature guarantees that the iteration will complete within a fixed time bound, which can be verified by static analysis of the generated code.
· Portability: Because the compiler emits standard C/C++, the same source can be compiled for different architectures (x86, ARM, RISC‑V) with the same performance guarantees, provided the target has the required accelerator capabilities.
· Security and Auditability: The entire stack is visible and verifiable. There is no hidden runtime state; the behaviour is fully determined by the input graph and the compiler algorithms. This is attractive for regulated industries.

Thus, while this dissertation focuses on the theoretical foundations, it is explicitly designed to enable a concrete C/C++ implementation. The theorems guarantee that such an implementation will—when the model assumptions hold—achieve the predicted performance without empirical tuning.

---

7.4 Implications and Limitations

7.4.1 The End of Dynamic Overheads

The unified theorem implies that a fully static training stack can match or exceed the performance of any dynamic runtime system. Dynamic frameworks (PyTorch, TensorFlow eager, etc.) incur overheads from runtime tape construction, heuristic scheduling, and garbage‑collected memory management. Our results demonstrate that all of these tasks can be shifted to compile‑time, and the resulting static schedule and memory plan are provably optimal. The use of C/C++ as the implementation language makes this shift not only feasible but also natural.

7.4.2 Limits of the Theoretical Model

The assumptions of the model must be acknowledged:

· Homogeneity: All stages have equal performance; all compute operations of a given type have identical duration. Real hardware exhibits variance (e.g., different layer latencies, core frequency boosting). The static schedule can incorporate measured worst‑case times, but may lose some efficiency.
· Determinism: The model requires deterministic operations. Random components (dropout, input shuffling) must be treated as fixed seeds or pre‑computed noise tensors, which is standard in reproducible research but requires care.
· Fault‑free execution: The analysis ignores hardware faults. In a real deployment, check‑pointing and recovery mechanisms (themselves static) would need to be added.
· Linear topology: The current theory is restricted to pipeline parallelism; extensions to hybrid model‑data parallelism remain future work.

Within these bounds, the framework provides an absolute performance ceiling that any implementation can strive for.

7.4.3 Generality of the Techniques

The underlying formalisms—timed dataflow graphs, interval memory minimisation, and flow‑shop scheduling—are broadly applicable. The credit‑based buffer analysis and the proper‑interval property of pipeline schedules can be reused for other streaming computations. The graph‑rewriting approach of Stencil‑AD is a template for any compiler that wishes to inline communication into a differentiable computation.

---

7.5 Extensions and Future Work

The theoretical results presented here open numerous avenues for further investigation.

7.5.1 General DAG Parallelism

Extending the framework to arbitrary model‑parallel topologies (e.g., encoder‑decoder, Mixture‑of‑Experts) requires a multi‑graph scheduling theory that handles branching and heterogeneous stages. For series‑parallel graphs, we conjecture that an optimal static schedule still exists and can be computed in polynomial time. For general DAGs, hardness results likely apply, but efficient approximation algorithms that exploit the regularity of neural architectures may be designed.

7.5.2 Dynamic Sparsity and Mixture‑of‑Experts

MoE layers introduce conditional compute, which breaks the static predictability assumption. A promising direction is stochastic scheduling with bounded over‑provisioning: the compiler statically reserves slots for a worst‑case expert load, with the guarantee that under any legal routing the schedule remains valid. Proving throughput bounds in this setting would require techniques from queueing theory and probabilistic resource allocation.

7.5.3 Fault‑Tolerant Static Compilation

At extreme scale, node failures are inevitable. The static schedule can be augmented with pre‑computed rescheduling plans: spare operations are mapped to idle slots (if they exist) or a contingency table is embedded in the binary to quickly redistribute work. Formalising this as a scheduling problem with preemption and recovery under uncertainty is an open theoretical challenge.

7.5.4 Numerical Precision Co‑design

Stencil‑AD’s recomputation relies on bit‑identical recomputation, which may not hold in reduced‑precision arithmetic. A theoretical extension could bound the gradient error introduced by lossy recomputation and integrate this bound into TopoMem’s cost model. This would allow the compiler to trade a controlled amount of numerical accuracy for extra memory savings, yielding a memory‑accuracy Pareto frontier.

7.5.5 A Complete C/C++ Compiler Prototype

A natural next step is to build BareMetalTrain as a proof‑of‑concept. The compiler would ingest an ONNX or TorchScript model, apply Stencil‑AD, run AOP and TopoMem, and emit a monolithic C++ source that uses CUDA or OpenCL for accelerators. Even a limited prototype—targeting a single pipeline configuration—would validate the feasibility of the theoretical blueprint and serve as a research platform for the extensions above.

