This chapter presented the Unified Optimality Theorem, the capstone of the dissertation. It proves that a static training stack combining Stencil‑AD, AOP, and TopoMem simultaneously achieves correct gradients, zero bubble, and minimal peak memory. The discussion then bridged theory and practice, arguing that C and C++ are ideal implementation languages for realising this framework in a real compiler. The resulting system would be a self‑contained, predictable, and high‑performance training binary, embodying the theoretical limits derived in this work. We closed by outlining a rich landscape of future research, from general DAG parallelism to a full compiler prototype. The foundation laid here offers a new paradigm for deep learning infrastructure: compile once, train optimally everywhere.

