Appendix A: Notation and Core Definitions

A.1 General Notation

· \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} – non‑negative real numbers.
· P – number of pipeline stages (devices).
· M – number of micro‑batches.
· \tau – duration of a single compute operation in the unit‑time model.
· T_F, T_{BI}, T_{BW} – durations of forward, backward‑input, backward‑weight passes.
· \mathcal{A} – Stencil‑AD transformation.
· \mathcal{G} – a BG‑IR graph.

A.2 BG‑IR Formal Definition

A BG‑IR is an 8‑tuple

G = (V, E, \tau, \delta, d, \kappa_V, \kappa_E, \sigma)

where
V – finite set of nodes,
d: V \to \{1,\dots,P\} – stage assignment,
\kappa_V: V \to \{\texttt{compute}, \texttt{send}, \texttt{recv}, \texttt{alloc}, \texttt{free}\},
\tau: V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} – duration (compute) or data volume (communication),
\sigma: V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} – size of allocated tensor (for alloc nodes),
E \subseteq V \times V – edges,
\kappa_E: E \to \{\texttt{data}, \texttt{gradient}, \texttt{memory}, \texttt{comm\_order}\},
\delta: E \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} – edge latency.

A valid schedule S: V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} must satisfy edge constraints (2.1), device mutual exclusion (2.2), link buffer capacity (2.3), and device memory capacity (2.4) as defined in Chapter 2.

