Appendix B: Proofs of Hardness Results

B.1 NP‑Hardness of PIPELINE‑SCHED (Theorem 2.2)

Full reduction.
We reduce from RCPSP with a single renewable resource (memory) and a machine resource of capacity 1.

RCPSP instance:

· Jobs j=1,\dots,n, duration p_j, memory demand r_j.
· Precedence constraints \prec.
· Memory capacity R, machine capacity 1.
· Deadline D.

Construction:

· P = 1.
· For each job j, create:
  · \texttt{alloc}_j with \sigma = r_j, \tau=0.
  · \texttt{compute}_j with \tau = p_j.
  · \texttt{free}_j with \tau=0.
· Edges: \texttt{alloc}_j \to \texttt{compute}_j, \texttt{compute}_j \to \texttt{free}_j (memory edges).
· If j \prec k, add edge \texttt{free}_j \to \texttt{alloc}_k (data edge, \delta=0).
· Set B_{\text{mem}}(1) = R.
· Target makespan T = D.

Equivalence:
(⇒) Given a valid BG‑IR schedule, define s_j = S(\texttt{compute}_j). Edge constraints enforce precedence. Memory constraint (2.4) during the live interval of job j (from S(\texttt{alloc}_j) to S(\texttt{free}_j)) ensures the sum of r_j over concurrently running jobs ≤ R. Device mutual exclusion (2.2) guarantees at most one compute node runs at a time, satisfying machine capacity. Makespan ≤ D matches deadline.

(⇐) From a feasible RCPSP schedule s_j, set S(\texttt{alloc}_j) = s_j, S(\texttt{compute}_j) = s_j, S(\texttt{free}_j) = s_j + p_j. All BG‑IR constraints hold because job intervals are non‑overlapping and resource usage respects R. ∎

B.2 NP‑Hardness of PMM (Theorem 4.1)

We note that PMM contains Dynamic Storage Allocation (DSA) as a special case: a single stage, all activations produced at time 0, last used at time T, DMA is the single resource. The problem of placing offload/reload intervals to minimise peak memory is exactly DSA, which is strongly NP‑complete (Garey & Johnson, 1979). Hence PMM is NP‑hard. A full reduction from 3‑Partition can be written along standard lines.

