Appendix D: TopoMem Exact Algorithm and Optimality Proofs

D.1 The TopoMem Algorithm

```
Algorithm TopoMem (Exact)
Input: Stage with fixed schedule, idle intervals I = {[u_j, v_j]}, activation set A,
       sizes s_a, lifetimes [prod_a, last_a], DMA bandwidth b.
Output: Minimal peak memory and offload plan.

1. Compute baseline memory profile L(t) for t ∈ [0, T].
   P_base = max_t L(t).
2. Total idle time T_idle = Σ (v_j - u_j).
3. Sort A in descending order of s_a.
4. For each a in sorted order:
       if 2 * s_a / b ≤ T_idle:
           subtract s_a from L(t) for all t ∈ [prod_a, last_a]
           T_idle -= 2 * s_a / b
           mark a as offloaded
5. New peak P_new = max_t L(t).
6. Schedule offloads/reloads into idle windows using any feasible packing
   (e.g., earliest deadline first). Because T_idle was sufficient, packing succeeds.
7. Return P_new and the transfer schedule.
```

D.2 Optimality Proof for TopoMem on Proper Intervals

Lemma D.1 (Proper Interval Property). In the AOP schedule (and 1F1B), for any two activations a,b on the same stage, if prod_a < prod_b then last_a \le last_b. Thus the live intervals do not cross; they form a set of proper intervals.

Proof. By construction of the schedule, forward passes execute in micro‑batch order, and backward passes of earlier micro‑batches finish before later ones. The intervals are nested according to micro‑batch index. ∎

Theorem D.2 (Optimality). TopoMem finds the minimum peak memory achievable by offloading, given the idle intervals and the proper interval property.

Proof. (Sketch: exchange argument)
Let \mathcal{O} be an optimal set of offloaded tensors. We may assume \mathcal{O} is maximal with respect to idle time: if there exists a tensor that can still be offloaded without violating idle time, \mathcal{O} would include it because offloading never increases peak memory. Now sort \mathcal{O} by size and compare with the greedy set \mathcal{G}. Suppose the first difference is at tensor a: a \in \mathcal{G} but a \notin \mathcal{O}. Because \mathcal{O} is optimal and peak memory is determined by the sum of sizes of tensors covering the peak time(s), and offloading a tensor removes its size from the whole interval, swapping a smaller offloaded tensor for a would decrease (or keep same) peak memory and use no more idle time. Repeating yields \mathcal{G}. Hence \mathcal{G} is optimal. ∎

