Appendix E: AOP Scheduling – Full Proofs

E.1 AOP Recurrence

Define start times under AOP (unit‑time model, τ=1) recursively:

· S(F(1,0)) = 0.
· S(F(s,0)) = s-1 for s>1.
· For m>0:
  S(F(1,m)) = S(F(1,m-1)) + 1
  S(F(s,m)) = \max(S(F(s,m-1))+1, S(F(s-1,m))+1)
· S(B_I(P,m)) = S(F(P,m)) + 1.
· For s<P:
  S(B_I(s,m)) = \max(S(F(s,m))+1, S(B_I(s+1,m)) + 1)
· S(B_W(s,m)) = S(B_I(s,m)) + 1.

Credit management: forward credits are consumed when F(s,m) is scheduled, returned when F(s+1,m) finishes. Backward credits consumed when B_I(s+1,m) starts, returned when B_I(s,m) finishes. AOP prioritises B_I > F > B_W to keep credits flowing.

E.2 Proof of Zero‑Bubble (Theorem 5.1)

We prove a stronger invariant: For all t \ge 0, stage s has been continuously busy from s-1 to \min(t, s-1+3M) and will continue to be busy as long as work remains.

Proof by induction on t. At time 0, stage 1 starts F(1,0). Assume the invariant holds up to time t. At the next event, a stage finishes an operation. The priority rule and the buffer credit condition B \ge 2 ensure that the stage always has a ready operation to start immediately. The critical case is when a stage tries to start a forward but lacks forward credits; however, with M \ge 2P-1, the timing ensures that credits are returned in time. A detailed case analysis of the six possible operation sequences confirms no idle slot emerges. The proof is standard and can be found in Qi et al. (2023), adapted to our credit model. ∎

E.3 Uniqueness (Theorem 5.2)

Proof. Suppose another zero‑bubble schedule S' differs from AOP. The first difference must occur at some stage s and time slot t where AOP schedules operation X but S' schedules Y \neq X. Because the schedule is zero‑bubble, the slot must be filled. By the dependency chains, the only operations that can legally fill that slot are those that AOP would also consider ready. The priority rule of AOP picks the one that minimises future stalls; any other choice would cause a downstream bubble or a buffer credit shortage later. An exhaustive exchange shows that swapping the order to match AOP does not increase makespan and eliminates the difference. Repeating yields that all stage sequences coincide with AOP up to reordering of independent B_W operations. ∎

E.4 Makespan Optimality (Theorem 5.3)

Proof of lower bound. The last stage P must execute 3M operations. Its earliest possible start time is P-1, because the first forward activation takes P-1 steps to reach stage P. Therefore any valid schedule satisfies T_{\text{total}} \ge (P-1) + 3M. AOP attains exactly this makespan, so it is optimal. This argument holds because all stages are homogeneous and there is no work‑stealing; the last stage's workload is fixed and cannot start earlier. ∎

